                  "rawText": "{\"schemaVersion\":\"operator-studio.agent-result/v1\",\"summary\":\"Rewrote run(inputs) to cut the elementwise chain from 5 allocating kernels (mul, mul, add, sub, relu) plus the redundant clone down to 3 kernels with a single allocation: a cached constant bias operand folded into an alpha-scaled add, an in-place accumulation of the y term, and an in-place clamp_min standing in for relu. get_inputs/get_test_cases/get_benchmark_inputs/reference are byte-for-byte unchanged.\",\"diagnosis\":{\"summary\":\"At 32x256 (8192 elements) the operator is launch-latency bound, not bandwidth bound: every eager ATen op is one kernel launch plus one caching-allocator allocation. Candidate-01 removed only the redundant .clone() (6 -> 5 kernels, 15.36us -> 9.12us), leaving the four-op expression x*1.25 + y*0.75 - 0.125 and its four intermediate allocations intact.\",\"bottlenecks\":[\"5 sequential kernel launches at ~1.8us each dominate the 9.12us candidate-01 latency\",\"4 throwaway intermediate allocations (mul, mul, add, sub) add caching-allocator CPU work on the critical path\",\"Non-fused relu forces a further full-tensor read+write pass\"]},\"patch\":\"diff --git a/run.py b/run.py\\n--- a/run.py\\n+++ b/run.py\\n@@ -21,7 +21,22 @@ def get_benchmark_inputs():\\n def reference(inputs):\\n     return torch.relu(inputs['x'] * 1.25 + inputs['y'] * 0.75 - 0.125)\\n \\n+_CONST_CACHE = {}\\n+\\n+def _bias_tensor(x, value):\\n+    # Cached constant operand so the bias term costs no extra kernel launch.\\n+    key = (tuple(x.shape), x.dtype, x.device)\\n+    const = _CONST_CACHE.get(key)\\n+    if const is None:\\n+        const = torch.full(key[0], value, dtype=x.dtype, device=x.device)\\n+        _CONST_CACHE[key] = const\\n+    return const\\n+\\n def run(inputs):\\n-    # Initial implementation deliberately does one redundant device copy.\\n-    value = torch.relu(inputs['x'] * 1.25 + inputs['y'] * 0.75 - 0.125)\\n-    return value.clone()\\n+    # 1.25*x + 0.75*y - 0.125, launched as three in-place-friendly kernels:\\n+    #   bias + 1.25*x  ->  += 0.75*y  ->  clamp_min(0)\\n+    x = inputs['x']\\n+    y = inputs['y']\\n+    out = torch.add(_bias_tensor(x, -0.125), x, alpha=1.25)\\n+    out.add_(y, alpha=0.75)\\n+    out.clamp_min_(0.0)\\n+    return out\\n\",\"candidates\":[{\"id\":\"candidate-02\",\"title\":\"3-kernel fused affine+relu with cached bias and in-place accumulation\",\"hypothesis\":\"The 32x256 operator is launch-bound, so collapsing the 5-kernel expression (x*1.25, y*0.75, add, sub, relu) into 3 kernels — one allocation for (bias + 1.25*x), an in-place += 0.75*y, and an in-place clamp_min(0) — removes two launches and three intermediate allocations from the measured path, giving a strictly lower p50 than the 5-kernel candidate-01 (9.12us).\",\"change\":\"run(inputs) now builds out = torch.add(cached_const(-0.125), x, alpha=1.25), then out.add_(y, alpha=0.75), then out.clamp_min_(0.0) and returns out. The -0.125 bias lives in a per-(shape,dtype,device) cached constant tensor so it costs no kernel and no per-call allocation. Inputs are never mutated (the constant is only read as an out-of-place operand), so repeated benchmark calls and the reference oracle see unchanged data.\",\"files\":[\"run.py\"],\"sourceReferences\":[],\"risks\":[\"Float summation order differs from reference ((bias+1.25x)+0.75y vs 1.25x+0.75y-bias); worst-case deviation ~1e-6 absolute against values up to 10, far inside atol=rtol=1e-5\",\"The cached constant is keyed by (shape, dtype, device); an unusual 0-dim or non-float input would fall back to a fresh torch.full, still correct\",\"out.add_/clamp_min_ operate only on the freshly allocated output tensor, never on inputs['x'] or inputs['y']\"]}],\"recommendedCandidate\":\"candidate-02\",\"nextAction\":{\"type\":\"candidate.plan\",\"title\":\"Queue candidate-02 for shared-GPU verification\",\"reason\":\"A real workspace Diff now exists for run.py in round 2; the previous candidate-01 was 9.12us against a 15.36us naive_v0 baseline and this Diff targets a different bottleneck (kernel-launch count) rather than the redundant copy.\",\"expectedOutput\":\"4/4 Correctness cases pass and primary-profile latency p50 measured below the 9.12us candidate-01 value\",\"risk\":\"low\"},\"risks\":[\"Absolute 99.9999% improvement over naive_v0 is unreachable for a launch-bound 8192-element op on this runner; the achievable gain is bounded by per-launch overhead (~1.8us/kernel saved)\",\"Verification is owned by the Operator Studio GPU queue; no local test, benchmark, dependency install, or network access was performed\"]}"
                },
