                    {
                      "id": "candidate-03",
                      "version": "agent.3",
                      "label": "3-kernel fused affine+relu with cached bias and in-place accumulation",
                      "title": "3-kernel fused affine+relu with cached bias and in-place accumulation",
                      "hypothesis": "The 32x256 operator is launch-bound, so collapsing the 5-kernel expression (x*1.25, y*0.75, add, sub, relu) into 3 kernels — one allocation for (bias + 1.25*x), an in-place += 0.75*y, and an in-place clamp_min(0) — removes two launches and three intermediate allocations from the measured path, giving a strictly lower p50 than the 5-kernel candidate-01 (9.12us).",
                      "change": "run(inputs) now builds out = torch.add(cached_const(-0.125), x, alpha=1.25), then out.add_(y, alpha=0.75), then out.clamp_min_(0.0) and returns out. The -0.125 bias lives in a per-(shape,dtype,device) cached constant tensor so it costs no kernel and no per-call allocation. Inputs are never mutated (the constant is only read as an out-of-place operand), so repeated benchmark calls and the reference oracle see unchanged data.",
                      "files": "run.py",
